{
  "requirement": "The source-provider interface MUST expose overridable parsing, text-search,\nand structural-search operations. The SDK MUST supply default implementations\nso a provider can support these operations by implementing resource listing\nand reading and using a supplied language registry.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.sdk.sources.provider_defaults`**\n1. The provider interface exposes overridable parse, text-search and structural-search operations.\n2. The SDK supplies defaults for all three. A provider that implements only `list` and `read` gets them, and the structural default uses a supplied language registry.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **Default parse and text search:** `tests/sources.rs#fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]` uses `Memory`, a provider with only `list` and `read`. It calls the default `parse`, then the default `search_text`.\n  - For `parse`, it asserts the parsed text and outline come from one read, and that a later read does not change the snapshot.\n  - For `search_text`, it asserts match count, line, excerpt with context, completeness, and the exact sequence of `list` and `read` calls.\n- **Default structural search with a registry:** `tests/sources.rs#fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` uses `Primitives`, which implements only `list` and `read`.\n  - It asserts that the default `parse` and `search_structure` work with the supplied registry.\n  - It asserts the match selector, excerpt and resource.\n  - A language mismatch gives an empty, complete result. An unknown language gives an error.\n  - An explicit language beats the extension, and an implicit one is picked from the extension.\n- **Overridability:** `tests/sources.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` uses `CachedParse`, whose `read` always errors but which overrides `parse`.\n  - Default `search_structure` still succeeds, so it dispatches through the overridden `parse`.\n  - The test checks limits, completeness, scope filtering, and that `parse` ran exactly three times.\n- **Other marked tests:** `tests/agent.rs#fn[source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads]` also exercises the defaults end to end through `SourceAnalysis<LocalSources>`.\n\n**Residual point (not a gap)**\nNo test overrides `search_text` or `search_structure` themselves. In `src/sources.rs#trait[SourceProvider]`, all three operations are declared as trait methods whose bodies only delegate to the `defaults` functions. Overridability of those methods is a compile-time Rust property with no conditional or transformation of its own. The one override that carries runtime behavior, `parse`, is exercised by `CachedParse`, because the default `search_structure` calls `provider.parse`. I treat this as not a gap.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gap.",
  "references": "f81af2930dbcb6e08cb02303b0bab8c8c984cacdc898527c7b3e2bc6e3c399b9",
